{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1043 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "37bdd6758fedc9edc7367318d0b1d536b4fe841867ae9ed058019ad7b52afde4" :edb/db-before-t 1042 :edb/db-after-t 1043 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534355 50 #inst "2026-10-11T19:34:13.905Z" 13194139534355 17592186045417 true] [17592186158798 1078 #edb/ref 17592186158801 13194139534355 17592186045417 true] [17592186162056 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420136 13194139534355 17592186045417 true] [17592186162056 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420139 13194139534355 17592186045417 true] [17592186162057 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420140 13194139534355 17592186045417 true] [17592186162058 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420137 13194139534355 17592186045417 true] [17592186162058 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420138 13194139534355 17592186045417 true] [17592186162181 1050 420.0 13194139534355 17592186045417 true] [17592186162263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186158780 13194139534355 17592186045417 false] [17592186162266 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534355 17592186045417 true] [17592186162266 1071 "The probability distribution of the random variable $X$ is given below. Given that $E[X] = 7$, calculate $Var[5X]$.\n\n| $x$ | $5$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139534355 17592186045417 false] [17592186162266 1071 "The probability distribution of the random variable $X$ is given below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=7,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[5X].$ \n\n| $x$ | $5$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ |" 13194139534355 17592186045417 true] [17592186162266 1074 "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[5X] & ={5}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =25\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the variance of $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given that $\\text{E}[X]=7.$ \n\nWe start by computing $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={5}^{2}\\cdot f(5)+{6}^{2}\\cdot f(6)+{9}^{2}\\cdot f(9) \\\\  & =25(\\frac{1}{5})+36(\\frac{2}{5})+81(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{259}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{259}{5}-{7}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{259}{5}-49 \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[5X] & =25\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =25\\cdot \\frac{14}{5} \\\\  & =14\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =70.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534355 17592186045417 true] [17592186163273 1064 "There are several important properties of the variance of a random variable that we can use to simplify expressions.\n\nFirstly, the variance of a constant is always zero. That is, for any constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\\textrm{Var}[a] = 0.$$\n\nIntuitively, this makes sense. A constant does not vary, so the mean deviation from its expected value should be zero." 13194139534355 17592186045417 false] [17592186163273 1064 "There are several important properties of the variance of a random variable that we can use to simplify expressions. \n\nFirstly, the variance of a constant is always zero. That is, for any constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[a] = 0.\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this makes sense. A constant does not vary, so the mean deviation from its expected value should be zero." 13194139534355 17592186045417 true] [17592186163274 1064 "Suppose that $X$ is a random variable and $a$ is a constant. Then,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nSo, for example, if we know that $\\textrm{Var}[X] = 2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[3X] & ={3}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =9\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[-4X] & =(-4)^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =16\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =16\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** To understand why we have to square the constant when we factor it outside the variance, it's helpful to remember that the variance is defined as the expected *squared* deviation from the mean:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [ (X - \\textrm E[X])^2 ]\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[aX] & =\\text{E}[(aX-\\text{E}[aX])^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[(aX-a\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{a}^{2}(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & ={a}^{2}\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & ={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534355 17592186045417 true] [17592186163274 1064 "Suppose that $X$ is a random variable and $a$ is a constant. Then,\n\n$$\\textrm{Var}[aX] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X].$$\n\nSo, for example, if we know that $\\textrm{Var}[X] = 2,$ then\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[3X] & ={3}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\ & =9\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\ & =9\\cdot 2 \\\\ & =18.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[-4X] & =(-4)^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\ & =16\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\ & =16\\cdot 2 \\\\ & =32.\\end{aligned}$$\n\n**Note:** To understand why we have to square the constant when we factor it outside the variance, it's helpful to remember that the variance is defined as the expected *squared* deviation from the mean:\n\n$$\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [ (X - \\textrm E[X])^2 ]$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[aX] & =\\text{E}[(aX-\\text{E}[aX])^{2}] \\\\ & =\\text{E}[(aX-a\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\ & =\\text{E}[{a}^{2}(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\ & ={a}^{2}\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\ & ={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}$$" 13194139534355 17592186045417 false] [17592186163275 1064 "Suppose that $X$ is a random variable and $a$ and $b$ are constants. It can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX+b] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nA proof of this result is given at the end of this lesson.\n\nTherefore, when a random variable is scaled by a factor of $a$ and shifted by a constant $b\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n- the variance is scaled by a factor of $a^2$ as before, and\n\n- the variance is unaffected by the shift.\n\nThe fact that shifting a random variable leaves the variance unaltered makes intuitive sense. Shifting all values of $X$ by a fixed amount does not change how far apart those values are from each other. \n\nFor example, if we know that $\\textrm{Var}[X] = 3,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[2X+1] & ={2}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534355 17592186045417 true] [17592186163275 1064 "Suppose that $X$ is a random variable and $a$ and $b$ are constants. It can be shown that\n\n$$\\textrm{Var}[aX+b] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X].$$\n\nA proof of this result is given at the end of this lesson.\n\nTherefore, when a random variable is scaled by a factor of $a$ and shifted by a constant $b\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- the variance is scaled by a factor of $a^2$ as before, and\n- the variance is unaffected by the shift.\n\nThe fact that shifting a random variable leaves the variance unaltered makes intuitive sense. Shifting all values of $X$ by a fixed amount does not change how far apart those values are from each other.\n\nFor example, if we know that $\\textrm{Var}[X] = 3,$ then\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[2X+1] & ={2}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\ & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\ & =4\\cdot 3 \\\\ & =12.\\end{aligned}$$" 13194139534355 17592186045417 false] [17592186163276 1064 "We wish to prove the following result:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX+b] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X]\n$$\n\nRecall that, by definition,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [ (X - \\textrm E[X])^2 ].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[aX+b] & =\\text{E}[(aX+b-\\text{E}[aX+b])^{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expected value, we can simplify this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[(aX+b-\\text{E}[aX+b])^{2}] & =\\text{E}[(aX+b-a\\text{E}[X]-b)^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[(aX-a\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[(a(X-\\text{E}[X]))^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{a}^{2}(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & ={a}^{2}\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & ={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534355 17592186045417 true] [17592186163276 1064 "We wish to prove the following result:\n\n$$\\textrm{Var}[aX+b] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X]$$\n\nRecall that, by definition,\n\n$$\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [ (X - \\textrm E[X])^2 ].$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[aX+b] & =\\text{E}[(aX+b-\\text{E}[aX+b])^{2}].\\end{aligned}$$\n\nUsing the properties of expected value, we can simplify this as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[(aX+b-\\text{E}[aX+b])^{2}] & =\\text{E}[(aX+b-a\\text{E}[X]-b)^{2}] \\\\ & =\\text{E}[(aX-a\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\ & =\\text{E}[(a(X-\\text{E}[X]))^{2}] \\\\ & =\\text{E}[{a}^{2}(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\ & ={a}^{2}\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\ & ={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]\\end{aligned}$$" 13194139534355 17592186045417 false] [17592186420106 1080 "5" 13194139534355 17592186045417 true] [17592186420106 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534355 17592186045417 true] [17592186420106 1083 #uuid "02b65569-fec4-5208-a935-babc35b50772" 13194139534355 17592186045417 true] [17592186420107 1080 "\\frac{15}{8}" 13194139534355 17592186045417 true] [17592186420107 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534355 17592186045417 true] [17592186420107 1083 #uuid "18fcdc20-d9ce-5918-a799-224704442b0d" 13194139534355 17592186045417 true] [17592186420108 1080 "\\frac{1}{c}" 13194139534355 17592186045417 true] [17592186420108 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534355 17592186045417 true] [17592186420108 1083 #uuid "245deae8-2edb-5248-9f72-3066a50940b3" 13194139534355 1759